{
  "concept": "The Frasch process extracts underground sulfur without mining by using three concentric pipes: superheated water melts the deposit, compressed air lifts the molten sulfur, and the mixture rises to the surface.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "three concentric vertical pipes (outer, middle, innermost) extending from above ground into the deposit",
    "above-ground surface region at top",
    "brown soil layer, labeled 'Soil'",
    "yellow solid sulfur deposit layer, labeled 'Solid sulfur deposit'",
    "pool of liquid sulfur at the pipe base, labeled 'Liquid sulfur'",
    "downward arrows in the outer pipe labeled 'Superheated water'",
    "downward arrows in the innermost pipe labeled 'Compressed air' entering upper right",
    "upward arrows in the middle pipe carrying the mixture, exiting upper left labeled 'Sulfur, water, and air'",
    "curved manifold at pipe base directing water outward and molten sulfur inward"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "flowAnimation",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "Play process flow",
      "loop": true,
      "teaches": "The three fluid streams run simultaneously and continuously: hot water descends and melts sulfur, air descends and aerates the melt, and the buoyant froth is pushed up the middle pipe — a closed, self-sustaining extraction cycle"
    },
    {
      "id": "phaseStep",
      "type": "state",
      "label": "Step through phases",
      "states": ["melt", "aerate", "lift"],
      "default": "melt",
      "teaches": "Each pipe has one distinct job — melting (outer), lifting force (inner air), and product recovery (middle) — and isolating each phase shows why all three are needed together"
    },
    {
      "id": "labelHover",
      "type": "hover",
      "teaches": "Hovering each pipe or stratum reveals its role and conditions (e.g. water at ~170 °C under pressure, sulfur's low melting point) without cluttering the diagram"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Melting the deposit",
      "narration": "Superheated water pumped down the outer pipe flows out at the base and melts the surrounding solid sulfur, which collects as a liquid pool.",
      "state": { "phaseStep": "melt" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Injecting compressed air",
      "narration": "Compressed air descends the innermost pipe and mixes into the molten sulfur, forming a low-density froth.",
      "state": { "phaseStep": "aerate" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Lifting the sulfur",
      "narration": "The air-sulfur-water froth is light enough to be forced up the middle pipe and out at the surface — no miners ever go underground.",
      "state": { "phaseStep": "lift" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Sulfur is extracted entirely from the surface because superheated water exploits its low melting point and compressed air exploits its low density — three concentric pipes turn melting, lifting, and recovery into one continuous physical process.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Vertical cutaway: three nested pipe walls drawn as thick black outlines, with the outer pipe descending into the yellow sulfur deposit at bottom. Above ground the pipes bend into horizontal ports: compressed air in from upper right, superheated water in from mid-right, mixture out upper left. Brown soil band across the middle, yellow deposit below, with a slightly darker liquid-sulfur pool at the pipe base. Flow arrows: downward in the outer and innermost pipes, upward in the middle pipe, curving at the bottom manifold. Animate arrows/particles moving along each path; highlight one phase's path at a time in the stepped mode (e.g. water path in orange-red, air in light blue, mixture in yellow). Labels in plain sans-serif positioned as in the original."
}

